On 2008-12-27, Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
> I'd like people's opinions and reactions to this laundry 
> list of game design choices.

These will of course be from my perspective as designer.

> * First and biggest?  For personal and moral reasons, I'd 
>   refuse to put in items, monsters, and artifacts that are 
>   subject to existing copyrights...

I don't see this as even worth caring about, so I wouldn't care whether
you had them in or out.

> * Magic markers probably wouldn't exist. They are almost
>   always detrimental to the game.  

Magic markers are something I haven't found a proper solution
for, honestly.  I'm generally inclined to say that if something is
broken in the first place, just making it more rare (by removing
markers) doesn't make it any less broken, it just makes it random.

To that effect I've already altered genocide scrolls, the primary
offender when used with markers.  Charging is less useful as well due to
random wands of wishing now being generated at 1:X.

I would also be more inclined to reduce the wizard's ridiculously high
chance of succesfully getting an unknown scroll or spellbook, rather instead
altering it so that the writer always got a working scroll/book... just
not necessarily the one they wanted.

This does/would reduce a lot of the problems with markers today; so in
general I would probably go with that, rather than simply dunk markers
completely.

> * New puddings (and any other creature that reproduces 
>   in dungeon realtime) would not be generated with new 
>   equipment.  No very strong feelings about summoned 
>   intelligent creatures, however. 

Doesn't solve the sacrificing/prayerscumming problem, but does solve the 
infinite items problem.

> * Wands of wishing would always have 2-3 charges, but 
>   would not recharge.  

Already did this.

>   They'd be rarer.  

And this.

>   Only one magic lamp per game would give a wish.  

They're just rarer now in shops (Izchak's was the prime offender).

>   Wishes for artifacts would be likely to fail. 

I went a different direction by upgrading most artifacts closer to
'ideal' and varying the powers significantly.  Since there are fewer
wishes in general and wishing for artifacts is somewhat limited anyway,
it requires better situational determination to select the 'best'
artifact at the time, rather than simply wishing up the Eye.

> * Artifact secondary powers (including resistances/
>   immunities in most artifacts) would be slightly randomized.  
>   Primary powers would be stable.  When you find a thing out 
>   of legend, you may find that the legend, while accurate 
>   in major points, has embellished a few details and gotten 
>   others wrong.

Crawl does this to a much greater degree.  It's not a bad thing.

> * AC from prayer would be limited in the same way that 
>   divine protection is limited now. In fact the limitation
>   might apply to their sum.

AC needs more of an overhaul than just this, but it's a pretty big task.
Realistically it needs to be thrown out and replaced with a completely
different system; there are many such tha would work.

> * Unicorn Horns would be nerfed somehow.  There are 
>   dozens of possible ways.

Already did this in one fashion.

> * Item curses cast by liches et al would mostly end when 
>   that particular lich gets destroyed.  

Making the game easier isn't so good though.  If you just have to kill
the thing that cursed your stuff, that really doesn't put much threat
into the curse itself; in fact, I'd totally ignore it, whereas currently
i'll actually take some care around cursers.

> * Blessing inventory would be increasingly unlikely to 
>   work if you had more than 4 or 5 pieces of blessed 
>   inventory already.  

...Does anyone bless anything by any means other than dipping it in holy
water?  Are you thinking of some mass-effect?  Does anyone bother
blessing their armor?  Or even their weapon, for that matter?

Plus, I'll just drop all my stuff on the Sokoban stairs and switch levels
while I bless each individual item.  Encouraging players to perform
tedious tasks because it's optimal goes the direction away from "fun".

> * No poisoned item could ever be blessed.  

Sure, but it won't make enough difference in effectiveness to matter
unless you also make blessing weapons be something significant.

> No character carrying poison or unholy water (or certain artifacts) 
> would be allowed to carry holy water at the same time. 

Too artificial.

> * No character carrying holy water (or certain other 
>   artifacts) would be allowed to carry unholy water or 
>   poison at the same time.

See above; too artificial.

If you want to provide penalties for acting "out of character", I'd
suggest making the act of _using_ these items carry a significant
alignment penalty (or bonus, depending), and possibly even physical
damage or god-anger, depending.  But just carrying them?  Bah.

> * I'd probably reduce the fooproofing subgame in other 
>   ways too, and reduce the consequences of not bothering
>   with it. I think it's boring.

I went the opposite direction: I provided actual consequences for not
taking care of your equipment, because there weren't any to reduce;
in vanilla a thoroughly rotted thoroughly burned +5 elven cloak provides
5 points of AC.  A fireproof +5 elven cloak provides 6.

Thus, using scrolls to fooproof most items is a waste of time.

> * Artifact "Blasting" of cross-aligned wielders wouldn't 
>   be on pickup only.  It would happen at random intervals 
>   during play, maybe double or triple prayer timeout 
>   intervals. Quest artifacts of particular classes might 
>   sometimes blast wielders of other classes.

This should be something other than just another way to lose HP.

> * #pray'ing while carrying a weapon or artifact whose 
>   alignment is opposed to your God's, or which is holy 
>   to some other God, might be a bit dangerous.  

Too corner-case: very hard to get such weapons or artifacts anyway.
Also subject to standard "drop, then pray" manipulation, though there
would be some circumstances where you wouldn't be able to do this.

